  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "1415384f91bfe7fbfb1a00316b74aec7219955d8",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 509294770,
          "type": null,
          "body": "So what is the error with those headers? Won't this change prevent the corresponding API from being callable in C++ code?",
          "author": {
            "id": 6722527,
            "username": "pquantin",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Pascal Quantin",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9bf49edc6acae0eca821eb90309381794fb4fbea4f3fb7c2378428862ddbb430?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/pquantin"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-02-15T14:23:06.519Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-02-15T14:23:06.519Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 88608167,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 2117,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "104342be8175d11470657072d7a42b35e90e3ad4",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 509319042,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I think what happens here is that for instance in epan_dissect.h you have extern \"C\"{... #include \"epan.h\" ... } and epan.h includes <glib.h> so even if I move <glib.h> outside of extern \"C\" in epan.h, it is still inside extern \"C\" through epan_dissect.h. \n\nTo be honest, I am not sure whether there is a better way to fix this but IMHO the usage of extern \"C\"{} in wireshark is a little bit of a mess. Here there are extern \"C\" around headers that only contain macros(some of the ws_* files), extern \"C\" around public header includes etc. This change that I did was a minimal change that fixed the problem.\n\nIIRC extern \"C\" should be around function declarations so that when compiling the code with C++, names of functions don't get mangled and can still be called from C code(at least the way I understand this, I might be wrong though). Using extern \"C\" inside headers around function declarations is OK but including whole headers should have some sort of justification or should not be used.",
          "author": {
            "id": 8167632,
            "username": "formaiko",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Michal Ruprich",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45679e68fd8476cd685d40b25e67a8b6401341499cf57cdeb255c35adac7d75e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/formaiko"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-02-15T14:44:45.494Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-02-15T14:44:45.494Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 88608167,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 2117,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a34b044f0216655b484dd0c60372828d66ff197e",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 509336985,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I agree with that. My feeling is that the current change is a hack rather than a real fix.\nI'm not sure anyone really used libwireshark API with C++ code. Should we perform a real pass on the header files so as to only have function declarations in the extern C brackets? We would do it by waves as it can be quite boring.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6722527,
            "username": "pquantin",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Pascal Quantin",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9bf49edc6acae0eca821eb90309381794fb4fbea4f3fb7c2378428862ddbb430?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/pquantin"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-02-15T14:59:48.536Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-02-15T14:59:48.536Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 88608167,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 2117,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "33e52c4284ddfa9eab4fcbaabdaa806e2f85debe",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 530214769,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I'm not sure anyone really used libwireshark API with C++ code.\n\nI have the impression that somebody out there built a Qt-based packet sniffer on top of libwireshark.  Anybody know more about that?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-03-16T08:57:42.158Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-03-16T08:57:42.158Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 88608167,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 2117,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "05bfd39617acd976bfee4fff3bd6ef3449ef186f",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 530976671,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> Should we perform a real pass on the header files so as to only have function declarations in the extern C brackets?\n\nThis whole `extern \"C\"` thing is a bit of a mess.  In the best of all possible worlds, there would be better *cross-language* calling sequence standards, and object file formats would support data types in symbol table entries so you don't need to decorate C++ function names (and so that linkers can do type checking...), and, if there's *really* a need for different calling sequences for different languages, C and C++ would both support `extern \"{language name}\"`, and it would be something applied to individual functions, the way other attributes such as `extern` and `static` and various compiler-dependent attributes are.\n\n*That* would be the fix.\n\nBut that's not the world we live in, so we're stuck with various flavors of ugliness and hackishness.\n\nHaving *only* function declarations in the brackets seems as ugly and hackish to me as excluding `#includes` from what's in the brackets; sometimes we have #defines, and structure declarations, and enum declarations, intermixed with function declarations.\n\nI've gone through and tried pulling as many `#includes` from there as possible; if somebody else is motivated to pull `#defines` and, if deemed appropriate, variable declarations from inside the extern C brackets, they can go ahead and do so, but I'm not motivated to do so.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-03-16T23:15:09.485Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-03-16T23:16:33.236Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 88608167,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 2117,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d66924870de6b46d2def1fa90c0004b7983a4469",
